## Potential of Microporous Metal-Organic Frameworks for Separation of Hydrocarbon Mixtures
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x
www.rsc.org/
In the process industries, the separation of mixtures of hydrocarbons is important both for preparation of feedstocks and for use as end products. The constituents hydrocarbons are either aliphatic or aromatic, saturated or unsaturated, with a large variation in the number of carbon atoms. Using microporous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a number of different separation strategies can be employed to achieve the desired separation performance. The strategies include selective binding with metal atoms of the framework, exploiting differences in molecular packing efficiencies within the ordered pore structures, utilizing selectivities based on framework flexibility and gate-opening mechanism, and molecular sieving. The various strategies are discussed in this article, along with perspectives for future research and development for improving the separation performance.
Broader context: While ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture in pre- and post- combustion processing, garners a lot of current research interest in the development of novel materials such as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), there is a much wider scope for utilizing MOFs for separation of hydrocarbon mixtures. The main purpose of this article to highlight the wide variety of separation strategies that are employable in this task. Particularly noteworthy is the exploitation of the differences in the stacking of aromatic molecules within the ordered crystalline MOF structures. Just to give one example, unlike traditionally used zeolitic materials, MOFs can be synthesized to yield one-dimensional triangular-shaped channels; this geometry offers unique separation possibilities that are not possible with other materials.
